.TH MU2E-POWER-REPORT 1 "2026-09-29" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.2.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
mu2e-power-report \- phase 4: the consolidated recovery report
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mu2e-power-report
.RB [ \-\-run\-id
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-post\-ecl ]
.RB [ \-\-publish ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs phase 4 of
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1):
it reads a finished run back out of the run store, renders that run's report
bundle, and optionally posts it to the Fermilab Electronic Logbook.
.PP
Run on its own it
.I regenerates
the report of an existing run \(em the one named by
.BR \-\-run\-id ,
or the newest. It attaches to that run rather than opening a new one: the
.B report
phase row and its events are recorded against the selected run, every page and
JSON file names that run, and the run's status and finish time are never
changed. It needs neither Kerberos nor Vault unless it posts.
.PP
This phase probes nothing, which is what makes it repeatable. An operator can
regenerate a report hours after the event, or repost it after fixing the
logbook credentials, without touching the cluster.
.PP
The narrative is derived entirely from stored rows, in the order a reader who
was not present needs them: what the run did, what is still wrong, and what has
to happen next. Current state is reconciled per node and check: the newest
result wins, a failure a later phase re-checked and passed is listed as
resolved rather than outstanding, and a failure nothing re-checked stays
outstanding (see
.B REPORT LIFECYCLE
in
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1)).
Follow-ups are derived rather than generic \(em a check that
failed on three or more nodes is called out as one likely shared cause rather
than as three separate problems.
.PP
Because the run store keeps each credential attempt, the report names the
identity that opened each node and, for a node nothing could open, each
login and ticket that was refused and why. A node whose host key had changed is
reported as that, not as a failed login.
.SH LOGBOOK POSTING
.B \-\-post\-ecl
posts the entry using the
.B ecl-client
package, which implements the ECL 8.x XML posting API. It is an optional
dependency: if it is not installed, or the ECL is unreachable, the phase
records that in its notes and the HTML report \(em the durable artefact \(em is
unaffected.
.PP
ECL credentials are read from Vault at the path given by
.I ecl.credential_path
under
.IR vault.base_path .
The Vault client is created only when posting, never by a report that is not
posted.
.PP
The post happens after the run's bundle,
.IR html/runs/<id>/ ,
has been rendered. With
.I ecl.attach_html
(the default) the entry carries that bundle's HTML pages \(em the detail page
included \(em and never a page from the shared top level, which may belong to a
newer run. The outcome is recorded as an event on the run and written into the
bundle's
.IR data/report.json .
A failed post leaves the complete local report in place and does not change the
exit status. Under
.B \-\-simulate
nothing is posted.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option of
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1)
is accepted except
.BR \-\-phase ,
which is fixed to
.BR report .
.SS Report
.TP
.BI \-\-run\-id " N"
Report on this run rather than the most recent one. Run ids are shown on the
report's run-history page and in the
.I run_id
field of
.BR "\-\-json" .
A run id not in the store exits 2 with a message on stderr; nothing is
written. The general driver accepts the flag only with
.BR "\-\-phase report" .
.TP
.B \-\-post\-ecl
Post the entry to the logbook.
.TP
.B \-\-publish
Copy the report site to
.I report.publish.target
after rendering.
.TP
.BI \-\-publish\-target " DEST"
Override the publication target, e.g.
.IR user@host:/web/recovery/ .
Implies
.BR \-\-publish .
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
Where the report pages are written. Default:
.IR html/ .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-report
Do not write the HTML pages. The phase still assembles the narrative, still
posts it if asked (with no attachments, since nothing was rendered), and still
returns its exit status; only the site on disk is skipped.
.SS Run control
.TP
.BI \-\-label " TEXT"
Accepted, and has no effect here: a regeneration opens no run, and the
report and logbook subject use the label stored with the selected run.
.TP
.B \-\-execute
Accepted, and has no effect: this phase issues no power command.
.TP
.B \-\-simulate
Contact nothing at all. The pages are written locally, nothing is posted to the
logbook, and the publisher refuses before trying anything, reporting that
nothing was published.
.TP
.BI \-\-location " NAME"
Repeatable. Accepted; it narrows the inventory page rather than any probe,
since this phase contacts nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-node " HOST"
Repeatable. Accepted; likewise nothing is probed.
.TP
.B \-\-continue\-on\-error
Accepted; it governs phase 2 and has no effect here.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " STAGE" ", " \-\-until " STAGE"
Accepted; they bound the phase-2 power-on sequence and have no effect here.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-failed
Accepted; it governs phase 3 and has no effect here.
.SS Credentials
.TP
.BI \-\-principal " PRINCIPAL"
Accepted; this phase opens no session of its own. Publication is a local
.BR rsync (1)
or
.BR scp (1)
run from the workstation, so a remote target is reached with the operator's
ordinary SSH credentials rather than through the run's credential chain.
.TP
.BI \-\-root\-principal " PRINCIPAL"
Accepted; not used by this phase.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-prompt
Never prompt for a password; fail with instructions instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-vault\-addr " URL"
HashiCorp Vault address. The ECL credentials are read from it, so
.B \-\-post\-ecl
needs it reachable.
.SS General
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the option summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print
.I mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.2.0
and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Main configuration file. Default:
.IR config/power-recovery.yaml .
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Dotenv file. Default:
.IR config/.env .
.TP
.BI \-\-database\-url " URL"
SQLAlchemy URL for the run store. Point it at the store that holds the run you
want reported.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-self\-update
Skip the phase-0 check for a newer revision.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-checks
Print every registered check and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-nodes
Print the node inventory and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Reserve stdout for exactly one JSON document; every human-readable line
(banner, mode notice, phase tables, notes, report and self-update messages, and
a self-update rebuild's output) goes to stderr. See
.B JSON OUTPUT
in
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Debug logging.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Warnings and errors only.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The report was generated and, if requested, posted.
.TP
.B 1
The report was generated but the run's reconciled current state contains
failures or unreachable nodes (or no node was assessed at all).
.TP
.B 2
No run could be found to report on: the store is empty, or
.B \-\-run\-id
names a run it does not hold.
.I run.dry_run: false
set only in configuration (YAML,
.I config/.env
or
.BR MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_RUN_DRY_RUN )
without
.B \-\-execute
or the
.B MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_ARM
token also exits 2 here, although this command issues no power command: the
authorisation check is the same for every entry point
(see
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR "LIVE RUNS" ).
A report-only run does not take the run lock, so it can be run while a
recovery is in progress.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-report
mu2e-power-report \-\-run\-id 17 \-\-post\-ecl
mu2e-power-report \-\-run\-id 17 \-\-json | jq .report.pages
mu2e-power-report \-\-publish \-\-publish\-target mu2edaq@mu2egateway01:/web/recovery/
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
